<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<package xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/packaging/2013/05/nuspec.xsd">
  <metadata>
    <id>VL.Overworld</id>
    <version>0.0.1-alpha</version>
    <title>VL.Overworld — spatial thinking for vvvv gamma, from geometry to maps</title>
    <authors>VL.Overworld Contributors</authors>
    <owners>VL.Overworld Contributors</owners>
    <requireLicenseAcceptance>false</requireLicenseAcceptance>
    <license type="expression">MIT</license>
    <projectUrl>https://github.com/rednotfound/VL.Overworld</projectUrl>
    <description>
      EARLY - a course, not a library.

      Spatial thinking for vvvv gamma, taught from the ground up in two acts: geometry first - points, paths and polygons answering questions under your cursor, offline - then the earth, with real maps and real data. This package contains no
      nodes of its own. It installs the libraries that do — VL.Mapsui to draw maps,
      VL.NetTopologySuite for geometry, VL.NetTopologySuite.GeoJSON to read and write it — and adds the chapters
      that show them working together.

      That is why it exists at all: a worthwhile spatial example needs several libraries at once, and
      no single library can declare the others without pretending to be something it is not.

      Starts from a dot under your cursor, and ends at thousands of features on a live map.
      No GIS background assumed; a working knowledge of vvvv gamma is.

      Requires vvvv gamma 7.4 or newer.
    </description>
    <summary>A course in spatial thinking for vvvv gamma, from geometry to maps. Installs the libraries it teaches.</summary>
    <releaseNotes>
      0.0.1-alpha - First package. Not published.
    </releaseNotes>
    <copyright>Copyright 2026 VL.Overworld Contributors</copyright>
    <!--
      The "VL" tag is what makes the pack discoverable at https://vvvv.org/packs. The rest are the
      words someone would actually type: nobody searches nuget for "overworld" - the name is a game
      word, chosen because this course teaches spatial thinking before cartography (see the third
      correction in docs\CURRICULUM.md) - so the tags carry the search, cartography included.
      "mapping" is avoided: in a graphics community it means projector warping.
    -->
    <tags>VL vvvv gamma gis geo geospatial spatial geometry map maps cartography overworld geojson coordinates projection mercator tutorial course</tags>
    <repository type="git" url="https://github.com/rednotfound/VL.Overworld" />

    <!--
      THE DEPENDENCIES ARE THE POINT OF THIS PACKAGE.

      Measured 2026-08-16: a nuspec <dependency> really is installed transitively, so one install
      of this brings the whole family. That is the thing a cross-library course can offer and an
      individual library cannot - VL.Mapsui must not depend on VL.NetTopologySuite.GeoJSON, because a map engine has
      no business requiring a GeoJSON reader, and the reverse is just as wrong.

      Versions are floors, not pins. A chapter that breaks against a newer library is a finding
      about the library, and the compile harness here is what surfaces it - this pack is a
      standing integration test that happens to also teach.
    -->
    <dependencies>
      <group targetFramework="net8.0">
        <dependency id="VL.Mapsui" version="0.0.2-alpha" />
        <dependency id="VL.NetTopologySuite.GeoJSON" version="0.0.2-alpha" />
        <dependency id="VL.NetTopologySuite" version="0.0.6-alpha" />
      </group>
    </dependencies>
  </metadata>

  <files>
    <!-- Entry point. Must sit at the package root, no subfolder, or vvvv never finds the package. -->
    <file src="VL.Overworld.vl" target="" />

    <!--
      No lib\ and no assembly, deliberately. This package contributes no nodes; every file it ships
      is a lesson. VL.ExtendedTutorials is the same shape and is the precedent.
    -->
    <file src="help\**\*.vl" target="help\" />
    <file src="help\Help.xml" target="help\" />

    <!--
      Sample data. Its own line because the glob above takes .vl and nothing else, and a chapter
      whose Path pin points at nothing is the quietest failure in this ecosystem. Every asset's
      licence is named in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md.
    -->
    <file src="help\Assets\**" target="help\Assets\" />

    <file src="README.md" target="docs\" />
    <file src="THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md" target="docs\" />

    <!--
      The curriculum's sources and licences ship too. README links to it, and a link that resolves
      in the repository but not inside the installed package is the quiet kind of broken - the
      reader who most wants "why is it in this order" is the one reading it offline.

      Note the flattening: everything here lands in docs\ side by side, so README's cross-links to
      its neighbours must be BARE FILENAMES, not docs\-prefixed paths. That is why the reference
      to this file from README is an absolute URL instead - it is the only form correct in both
      places at once.
    -->
    <file src="docs\CURRICULUM.md" target="docs\" />
  </files>
</package>
